oing an awful lot of damage which was  what J Edgar Hoover was famous for so  you really spent 16 years no I stopped  and did another book after my mother  died I did a book on illness loss in  grief and then came back to this book  and it kept haunting me and and I just  felt that I had to finish it what point  in time did I have stone jump from  regular journalist to superstar well  actually he had been he had he had about  three different periods in his life the  first period of his life was in the  early 30s and 40s and he was the  breaking scoop after scoop about Hitler  and about World War two and what was  going on with the cartels and p.m.  magazine which was a major New York  paper and also for the nation and then  he went into the weekly and started that  frankly because he didn't have a job as  he used to joke he said that on every  paper I worked I always thought they  would close on Monday and they always  did and so he had to find his own job  and what happened is he started in 53  which was the period of the McCarthy  witch hunts a terrible time for a lefty  journalist to start anything and he's  just incrementally crept up and crept up  and then he was the first journalist in  America to write about the gulf of  tonkin it was two weeks after LBJ said  that this was excuse for going into  Vietnam people don't remember this  timeframe this was a time when they said  that some shipping some little fishing  boats had had targeted one of our ships  and as he went through the documents as  he was always famous for doing and said  if there's no debris here what really  went on it's very very reminiscent of  what's happened with weapons of mass  destruction and of course he became a  superhero too the anti-war movement and  he became rich and famous and that was  in 1970 and 68 to 70 and then he started  writing a book on Socrates and  and had a best-seller at the age of 80  his life just kept going on it was the  great trajectory of a great American  story 